 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "CHALICE WELL. A spring that has never failed, running at about 25,000 gallons a day at the same cool temperature all year. The water runs red from the iron. Legend says the Holy Grail lies beneath it."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "THE TOR 158 m. Isle of Avalon in the legends. St Michael's Tower on the top is all that's left of a medieval church. Wear good boots."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 10,
   "text": "TO THE TOWER. Seven terraces wrap the hill, some say a medieval maze, some say farming. Either way, your calves will find out."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "GLASTONBURY ABBEY. A monastery for 1,000 years, said to be the oldest in England. In 1191 the monks announced they'd found King Arthur's grave. A convenient discovery."
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 37,
   "text": "THE GLASTONBURY THORN. Said to grow from Joseph of Arimathea's staff. This one flowers twice a year, in spring and at Christmas. It's a plant with excellent timing."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 40,
   "text": "THE ABBOT'S KITCHEN. Four fireplaces, a lantern roof, and a medieval workplace still standing. The abbot, famously, didn't wash up."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 32,
   "text": "THE FIELD. Glastonbury Festival, held at nearby Worthy Farm since 1970, when a ticket was a pound and included free milk. Today the milk is extra. The mud is still free."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "THE STONE CIRCLE. Modern, built for the festival, and still impressive. The real ones are at Avebury and Stonehenge. They won't be mentioned until later."
  }
 ],
